  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Implemented and independently approved the mathematical dispatch foundation without changing public matrix or polynomial behavior: constrained CPython declarations and deterministic JSON, closed typed schemas, pure explainable selection, capability and representation validation, specialization identities, portable/checked/local profiles, strict benchmark evidence ingestion, and robust adjacent threshold proposals. The dense prime matrix authority demonstrates GF(2), packed small-prime, FLINT resource, FFLAS/FFPACK, and typed-Python choices. Commits c755a23, 06ddd1e, df8f83b, and 775048e are pushed on origin/agent/math-dispatch-engine-v1.",
    "risks": [
      "This branch predates several shared compiler and native-artifact repairs. Its recorded full pnpm test:portable run passed all dispatch tests but failed seven inherited cases: missing optional FLINT addon artifacts, compiled-module isolation and dynamic-FFI fallout from that absence, and the separately diagnosed GF(2) native-bitwise marker regression.",
      "The checked-in portable thresholds are inspectable policy examples, not newly fitted authority from this lane; production host overlays still require correctness-stamped evidence on repaired Linux and Apple-silicon baselines.",
      "No public mathematical consumer uses this selector yet. Consumer integration must preserve existing semantics and record specialization identity in native cache keys before dispatch affects production execution."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Integrate these commits onto the repaired shared branch and rerun the complete portable tier with installed native artifacts.",
      "Wire one dense-prime operation through the selector in a separate claimed lane, preserving explicit override and explain output as the vertical slice.",
      "Collect comparable Linux x64 and macOS arm64 training and validation reports, then review generated checked profile proposals rather than hard-coding host crossovers."
    ]
  }
